{
  "infoMarkdown": "# maple\n\n## What this codebase does\n\nMaple is a multi-tenant OpenTelemetry observability platform with separate edge, ingestion,\nbackground-processing, local-mode, and agent-facing deployables.\n\n- The Rust ingest gateway accepts OTLP traces, logs, and metrics, browser replay and product\n  events, Cloudflare Logpush data, and optional OTLP gRPC traffic.\n- The Effect-based Cloudflare API exposes versioned product APIs, dashboard-internal APIs,\n  webhook receivers, SSE chat sessions, MCP tools, service-binding RPC, queues, and workflows.\n- Warehouse reads compile through Maple's ClickHouse query engine and execute against Tinybird,\n  managed ClickHouse, customer ClickHouse, or embedded chDB depending on tenant and mode.\n- The React dashboard, Electric shape proxy, and CLI consume those APIs; the CLI can also run a\n  local OTLP and SQL server backed by embedded chDB.\n- Cron workers evaluate alerts, anomalies, digests, integrations, retention, and AI-assisted error\n  investigations. A separate Slack agent invokes Maple through the authenticated MCP surface.\n\n## Auth shape\n\nAuthentication is deliberately different at each ingress boundary; there is no single global\nmiddleware covering every route family.\n\n- Product HTTP routes resolve `CurrentTenant` from Maple API keys, Clerk sessions, or self-hosted\n  session identities. V2 keys additionally carry scopes, and sensitive mutations check org roles.\n- MCP accepts tenant-scoped API keys, browser sessions, and a narrowly defined internal-service\n  mode. It installs `CurrentMcpTenant` before dispatch and rate-limits tool calls per principal.\n- Ingest resolves bearer or ingest-key headers through an HMAC-backed key store, then overwrites\n  client-supplied organization identity with the organization bound to that key.\n- Public webhook routes authenticate the raw request with Svix, GitHub-provider, PlanetScale, or\n  Slack signatures. OAuth callbacks instead rely on stored, expiring state.\n- Scraper and Slack service routes use timing-safe shared-bearer checks. Cloudflare RPC, queues,\n  cron events, workflows, and Durable Objects rely on platform binding isolation plus input decode.\n\n## Threat model\n\n- Internet clients control large compressed protobuf, JSON, NDJSON, replay, and metric payloads;\n  body, decompression, cardinality, parser, and downstream write limits are primary controls.\n- Cross-tenant access is the central authorization risk: org IDs appear in session IDs, headers,\n  queue jobs, RPC payloads, ClickHouse predicates, Postgres rows, cache keys, and agent context.\n- Telemetry text is attacker-influenced and later rendered in the dashboard or supplied to LLMs.\n  Treat log bodies, exceptions, span attributes, and tool output as prompt-injection and XSS input.\n- Scrape targets, integration APIs, OAuth providers, customer ClickHouse endpoints, and Logpush\n  connectors create outbound-request and credential-forwarding boundaries where SSRF matters.\n- Signed webhooks and asynchronous jobs may be replayed or delivered more than once; signature\n  freshness, idempotent persistence, queue decoding, retry limits, and terminal states need review.\n\n## Project-specific patterns to flag\n\n- Every warehouse query must carry an `OrgId` predicate. Verify that raw SQL, query-builder input,\n  cache keys, and service-binding RPC never bypass the executor's tenant enforcement.\n- Effect `HttpApi` routes receive schema decoding and typed failure mapping, while raw `HttpRouter`\n  routes such as SSE, OAuth, webhook, scraper, and MCP adapters must implement those checks manually.\n- Request-derived cursors, dates, bucket sizes, IDs, and query values should be constrained at the\n  HTTP boundary; compilation or decoding failures should not become generic 500 responses.\n- Internal RPC and queue bodies are only structured-cloned by Cloudflare unless explicitly decoded.\n  Treat caller-provided org IDs as trusted only after confirming the binding's caller set.\n- Review decrypted integration credentials, API-key secrets, Slack workspace caches, trace fields,\n  and error logs for accidental retention or cross-tenant reuse.\n\n## Known false-positives\n\n- `/health`, `/ready`, CORS preflight, discovery metadata, selected billing/catalog routes, and\n  signed OAuth or webhook callbacks are intentionally reachable without a user session.\n- The ingest onboarding sentinel intentionally acknowledges and discards payloads without resolving\n  a tenant; it is not a production ingest credential.\n- Expected ingest and scraper rejections in the 4xx range intentionally leave server spans `Ok`;\n  Maple follows the repository's OpenTelemetry status convention that only 5xx is `Error`.\n- The local CLI server intentionally permits loopback OTLP ingestion and arbitrary local SQL without\n  product authentication. Reassess this only when a non-loopback bind is allowed.\n- There are no Tinybird pipes or endpoints to audit. Domain endpoint declarations are type-only,\n  and production warehouse access is expected to pass through `WarehouseQueryService`.",
